and welcome to the
seventh enterprise community meetup,

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.475
where we talk about end to
end data science workflows with Posit Team.

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:12.935
My name is Simon Couch, and
I work on free and open source

00:00:12.935 --> 00:00:15.820
packages for machine
learning and r at Posit.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:17.720
In today's session,

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.880
I'm gonna walk through
an example of training and

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.740
deploying a machine
learning model.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.360
I'll be leaning heavily
on the tidymodels,

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.165
which is a framework for
machine learning and r that I

00:00:27.165 --> 00:00:29.345
work on to develop my model.

00:00:30.205 --> 00:00:30.845
Along the way,

00:00:30.845 --> 00:00:33.540
I'll show you how Posit Team
eases some of the pain points

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.640
of model development
that I encounter.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.260
During today's session,

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.805
I'll lay out three high level
steps for building a machine learning model.

00:00:41.805 --> 00:00:43.965
And then spend some time
with each of those steps

00:00:43.965 --> 00:00:47.005
individually before concluding with
resources where you can

00:00:47.005 --> 00:00:50.225
learn more about tidymodels
and about Posit team.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.940
After I present, we'll be
hanging out for a live Q and A.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.420
So please feel free to
ask questions as we go,

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:58.360
and I'm looking forward
to spending time.

00:00:59.355 --> 00:01:00.555
But with all that
out of the way,

00:01:00.555 --> 00:01:01.775
let's go ahead and get started.

00:01:02.955 --> 00:01:05.275
To talk about those
first and second steps,

00:01:05.275 --> 00:01:08.530
I'll make use of some slides.
And then for step three,

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:13.350
I'll drop into a live demo
using Posit Connect and Posit Workbench.

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:17.075
If you're interested in
spending more time with these

00:01:17.075 --> 00:01:20.295
slides or checking out the
source code behind this presentation,

00:01:21.155 --> 00:01:24.435
you can go to the link
in the footer of these slides. That's

00:01:26.597 --> 00:01:29.160
github.com/simonpcouch/mutagen.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:34.765
I've put together a diagram of
sort the high level process for

00:01:34.765 --> 00:01:36.785
developing a machine
learning model.

00:01:37.485 --> 00:01:40.690
And the three steps that I'm
talking about are kind of

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:44.950
called out with these three
hex stickers in the corners.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:50.565
In the top right corner, that
first step is to collect data

00:01:50.565 --> 00:01:52.185
and to spend time with it.

00:01:53.045 --> 00:01:56.290
Cleaning data, visualizing
it, understanding it,

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:59.570
are all important first steps
to building a machine learning

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:02.610
model. When I do
each of those steps,

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.595
I end up spending a lot
of time with the tidyverse packages.

00:02:05.595 --> 00:02:07.295
Which some of you
may have heard of.

00:02:08.875 --> 00:02:11.935
Once I move on to training
and evaluating models,

00:02:12.635 --> 00:02:16.700
I use the tidymodels packages, which
I work on day to day.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.935
And a common issue that
people run into when they're building

00:02:21.935 --> 00:02:26.415
models with tidymodels or
any model development software is,

00:02:26.415 --> 00:02:28.595
running out of
computational resources.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.080
If you're working in an
environment with lots of data

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.700
or you're trying to evaluate
really complex models,

00:02:36.535 --> 00:02:39.015
you can run out of
computational resources really

00:02:39.015 --> 00:02:44.990
quickly. And for me, this is a
place where Posit Workbench is really key.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:49.470
Recently I've started using it
when I run into this situation,

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:54.555
and it allows me to
drop into a computational environment where

00:02:54.555 --> 00:02:59.455
I have more resources and can
complete that model training more quickly.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:02.720
Once a model is trained,

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.520
it's time to get it into
the hands of people that will use

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.005
it. And that's where
the vetiver package comes in.

00:03:09.005 --> 00:03:13.005
Vetiver is free and open
source, and it provides tools

00:03:13.005 --> 00:03:17.310
to version models, deploy
models, and monitor them to

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:20.830
ensure that they continue to
perform as you expect them to

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:22.370
once they're out
into the world.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:27.925
Vetiver is tightly integrated
with Posit Connect. Which

00:03:27.925 --> 00:03:32.565
offers all sorts of tooling
for, getting the outputs of

00:03:32.565 --> 00:03:34.905
analysis into the right hands.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:40.380
So first, let's talk about
what do I even mean when I say,

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.040
machine learning or,
machine learning model?

00:03:44.805 --> 00:03:47.865
One example is a Netflix
movie recommendation.

00:03:48.885 --> 00:03:51.430
If you've ever gone to
somebody else's house,

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.670
and seeing the movies that
are recommended to them,

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.470
you might notice that they're
quite different than what you

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.090
might see at your own house.

00:03:58.845 --> 00:04:01.885
That's because the movies that
are recommended to you and the

00:04:01.885 --> 00:04:03.665
thumbnails that are part
of those recommendations

00:04:04.445 --> 00:04:06.760
are outputs of machine
learning modesl.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.120
And based on your viewing
history and the things that

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:11.740
you've said you're
interested in,

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:15.415
Netflix will recommend
different movies to you.

00:04:17.315 --> 00:04:18.055
Another example,

00:04:19.315 --> 00:04:22.220
on Zillow, if you're looking
at houses or apartments,

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.700
their estimates of the,

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:27.980
value of the property or the
rent that they would expect you

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:29.835
to pay if you were
to live there.

00:04:29.835 --> 00:04:32.655
So that's another example
of a machine learning model

00:04:33.755 --> 00:04:36.095
or email spam filters. So,

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.460
all of our emails whenever we
get an email at first, there's

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:44.700
a filter that looks at
the subject line. And if you've

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.925
received an email from
that person, before.

00:04:46.925 --> 00:04:49.965
And if there are any
typos inside of the email,

00:04:49.965 --> 00:04:52.225
and based on
information like that,

00:04:52.845 --> 00:04:56.160
a model will try to predict
whether an email is spam or

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.200
not. And if it does predict
that the email is spam,

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.740
it will go to a folder.

00:05:03.775 --> 00:05:07.695
So the thing that all of
these share is that they are guessing

00:05:07.695 --> 00:05:10.435
the value of an outcome
using predictors.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.270
For example, in the
email spam filters,

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:17.550
the outcome is whether
that email is spam or not.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:19.870
And the predictors are
those things I mentioned,

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:21.385
like the subject line,

00:05:21.385 --> 00:05:25.485
and the number of typos
inside and other things like that.

00:05:27.065 --> 00:05:28.985
For a Zillow property
value estimate,

00:05:28.985 --> 00:05:32.120
that might be something like
the square footage of the house

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.240
or the apartment. How
recently it was renovated?

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:36.620
What area it's in?

00:05:37.535 --> 00:05:39.695
And for Netflix movie
recommendations,

00:05:39.695 --> 00:05:43.055
that's things like your stated
interests in movies that you're

00:05:43.055 --> 00:05:46.740
interested in to how long
you spent watching movies in

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:47.640
different subcategories.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.420
Another example of a machine
learning model is one that this

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.120
presentation is based on.

00:05:54.885 --> 00:05:55.525
Day to day,

00:05:55.525 --> 00:05:59.065
my responsibility is just to
develop tools to build models.

00:05:59.765 --> 00:06:03.330
And so I often don't spend
a lot of time building models myself.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.910
But doing so helps us
better understand the perspectives of

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:10.750
our users, and so we try to
make time to really delve in on

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:13.135
a modeling problem
as often as we can.

00:06:13.675 --> 00:06:18.475
So recently, my colleague, Max Kuhn
and I spent some time to,

00:06:18.475 --> 00:06:23.010
develop a model and work through
the process to deploy it using,

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:26.130
Posit Workbench and Posit
Connect and wrote about that on

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:27.190
the Posit blog.

00:06:30.875 --> 00:06:32.075
In this blog post,

00:06:32.075 --> 00:06:34.955
we talk through a process
that's really similar to the

00:06:34.955 --> 00:06:38.315
one that I'll talk through
with you today where we explore

00:06:38.315 --> 00:06:42.280
data, we situate with our
modeling problem, we develop a

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:46.940
model to best understand that data,
and then we deploy it using Connect.

00:06:51.705 --> 00:06:54.345
So the process of machine
learning with Posit team, again,

00:06:54.345 --> 00:06:57.385
I'll just underscore those
three different sections before

00:06:57.385 --> 00:07:00.680
we dive in on them.
First, we'll spend some time to

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.260
understand and clean
data with the tidyverse,

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:08.140
and then we'll train and
evaluate models with tidymodels and Workbench.

00:07:08.825 --> 00:07:10.985
For both of these sections,
I'll just work through some

00:07:10.985 --> 00:07:14.585
slides. And if you're interested
in the details, you can,

00:07:14.585 --> 00:07:18.090
look at the source code of
the analysis and the source code

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:19.290
for these slides at

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.790
github.com/simonpcouch/mutagen.

00:07:25.405 --> 00:07:29.085
Once we've done so, we need
to get that model into the hands

00:07:29.085 --> 00:07:32.760
of users, and that's
where vetiver and Connect come

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.260
So let's start off
with understanding

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:39.180
and cleaning data.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:42.140
In pharmaceuticals,

00:07:42.745 --> 00:07:46.425
Mutagenicity refers to a drug's
tendency to increase the rate

00:07:46.425 --> 00:07:49.485
of mutations due to
damage caused to DNA.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:51.540
Mutagenicity

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:57.140
is a key indicator that a drug
may be a carcinogen or a cancer causing.

00:07:59.745 --> 00:08:04.225
Mutagenicity can be evaluated
with a lab test. So,

00:08:04.945 --> 00:08:07.765
a person can test whether
a drug is a mutagen,

00:08:08.545 --> 00:08:10.770
using a standard
wet lab procedure,

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:14.470
but those tests are costly
and those tests are time intensive.

00:08:16.935 --> 00:08:20.375
So a group of scientists
in our example problem converges to

00:08:20.375 --> 00:08:21.495
ask the question,

00:08:21.495 --> 00:08:23.815
what if a machine learning
model could predict

00:08:23.815 --> 00:08:26.475
mutagenicity using
known drug information?

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:31.800
That is if scientists are
spending time with a new drug,

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:35.565
using information that they
already have they can predict

00:08:35.565 --> 00:08:37.805
whether a drug is
a mutagen or not,

00:08:37.805 --> 00:08:40.445
which will allow them
to iterate more quickly when

00:08:40.445 --> 00:08:42.145
they're developing new drugs.

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:48.210
The source data that we'll be
working with is called mutagen table.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:53.665
It's a table with over
four thousand rows and notably

00:08:53.665 --> 00:08:55.365
over fifteen
hundred predictors.

00:08:56.065 --> 00:08:59.745
So in this first column, we
have whether the drug is a

00:08:59.745 --> 00:09:02.085
mutagen or whether
it's a non mutagen.

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:08.090
And for each of those compounds as
well, we have a whole lot of predictors,

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:12.195
giving us other information we
can use to predict whether a

00:09:12.195 --> 00:09:14.755
drug is a mutagen or not.

00:09:14.755 --> 00:09:16.915
So you're probably
thinking to yourself,

00:09:16.915 --> 00:09:19.955
if I have fifteen hundred
little pieces of information

00:09:19.955 --> 00:09:22.740
about this drug, it
surely must be pretty,

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:24.520
straightforward
to predict that.

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:28.920
It turns out it's not
quite that simple.

00:09:29.805 --> 00:09:33.425
On this plot, I've plotted
two of the predictors, and,

00:09:34.845 --> 00:09:38.125
each of those points is
also colored according to whether

00:09:38.125 --> 00:09:42.670
the drug is actually a mutagen
or whether it's a non mutagen.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:44.850
So colors in blue are mutagens.

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:47.570
Colors in yellow
are non mutagens.

00:09:48.395 --> 00:09:52.475
On the x axis, we have the
molecular weight and y axis is

00:09:52.475 --> 00:09:54.075
the partition coefficient.

00:09:54.075 --> 00:09:56.955
It doesn't matter whether you
understand either of these two

00:09:56.955 --> 00:10:00.360
predictors, I really don't
understand them. But,

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:03.800
the point here is that
it's really hard just using the

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:07.025
information we have here
to separate between the blue

00:10:07.025 --> 00:10:09.025
points and the yellow points,

00:10:09.025 --> 00:10:12.165
only knowing the value
of these two predictors.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:17.260
I can choose another
two of them. Right now,

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:21.580
I'm showing the number of
heavy atoms and the log of the

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:23.040
average valence connectivity.

00:10:24.925 --> 00:10:27.245
Whatever those two
predictors mean. Again,

00:10:27.245 --> 00:10:30.845
we're seeing that it's really hard
to sort of draw a line

00:10:30.845 --> 00:10:34.700
here. That would easily
separate the blue points and

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:35.660
the yellow points.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:39.885
And this is the goal that our
machine learning model will try to solve.

00:10:40.685 --> 00:10:43.585
So if you're like me,
you're feeling a little bit intimidated

00:10:44.205 --> 00:10:46.065
about tackling this.

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:54.210
This is where we move on to
the step of training and evaluating models.

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:58.065
So for this portion
of the presentation,

00:10:58.065 --> 00:11:01.185
instead of walking through the
notebook that does the training

00:11:01.185 --> 00:11:04.225
and the evaluating, I'll,

00:11:04.225 --> 00:11:07.270
leave it to folk to look
through the notebooks that are

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:09.770
on that GitHub page
after the presentation.

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:11.770
And instead

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:16.585
I'll try to answer the question
of why you might be interested

00:11:16.585 --> 00:11:21.245
in using tidymodels over other
modeling frameworks that are out there.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:27.060
The first reason why you should
be interested in using tidy

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:30.420
models is that tidymodels is
consistent in a way that other

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:33.385
modeling frameworks and
modeling packages are not.

00:11:34.965 --> 00:11:37.765
An example that I'll use
to call this out here,

00:11:37.765 --> 00:11:42.330
we can start off by showing
the code to fit a linear

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:45.950
model using the LM
function with tidymodels.

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:50.065
And a linear model using
only the LM function.

00:11:51.645 --> 00:11:54.605
On the left hand side,
there's just one line of code.

00:11:54.605 --> 00:11:57.390
We're using the example
MC card dataset here,

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:58.750
if you're familiar with it,

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:02.990
we're saying we want to
use all of the predictors on

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:05.410
in the mtcars data.

00:12:06.065 --> 00:12:10.565
To predict the outcome, which is
the miles per gallon, or MPG.

00:12:11.745 --> 00:12:14.325
And to do so, we'll
use the mtcars data.

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:19.470
On the right hand side,
the code does the same thing,

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:23.970
except the tidy model's wrapper has
been dropped on top of that function.

00:12:25.015 --> 00:12:27.755
The code reads will
use a linear regression

00:12:28.535 --> 00:12:32.075
using the LM function as
our computational engine

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.260
and we'll fit that model using,

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:39.660
the same dataset and the
same pre processing formula

00:12:40.325 --> 00:12:42.905
miles per gallon as a
function of everything.

00:12:44.165 --> 00:12:46.185
So when you see
this, you might think

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:50.560
it seems a whole lot easier
to just go ahead and use lm.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:53.780
It's only one line of
code, and I hear you.

00:12:54.615 --> 00:12:58.615
Where we really see the value
of tidymodels though is when we

00:12:58.615 --> 00:13:01.755
start to introduce complexity
into the modeling pipeline.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:05.840
So let's say you're using the
LM function and it's not doing

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:09.360
everything you need it to
do, and you want to add, for

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:10.500
example, a regularization

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:13.505
term to your linear regression.

00:13:13.505 --> 00:13:15.265
If you're not
familiar with that,

00:13:15.265 --> 00:13:19.105
doesn't matter to understand
what I'm about to demonstrate

00:13:19.105 --> 00:13:22.890
here. I want you to look
at that line three in

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:27.672
the tidy models portion of the code
where I've set the engine to LM.

00:13:27.672 --> 00:13:28.172
If

00:13:28.535 --> 00:13:31.975
I want to transition that
code to instead use Glmnet,

00:13:31.975 --> 00:13:36.750
all that I need to do is change
the the portion of the code

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:39.570
that said LM to
instead say Glmnet.

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:44.035
And you can see on
the left hand side,

00:13:45.715 --> 00:13:48.355
It was much more than just
one of the lines of code that

00:13:48.355 --> 00:13:49.415
needed to change.

00:13:50.195 --> 00:13:51.495
Instead here, Glmnet

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:55.960
for its first argument is going
to take all of the predictors

00:13:55.960 --> 00:14:00.915
and they can only be a
matrix. So thankfully here in mtcars.

00:14:00.915 --> 00:14:04.675
All of the predictors are
numbers anyway, and so they're

00:14:04.675 --> 00:14:08.980
one homogeneous type and we
can easily convert it with as.matrix.

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:14.500
And then instead of the name
of the outcome variable, like it

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:16.840
was in LM, the

00:14:17.695 --> 00:14:20.195
outcome here is
specified as the actual,

00:14:21.615 --> 00:14:25.250
vector of values in the
mtcars data set. So that's

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:27.350
mtcars$mpg.

00:14:29.330 --> 00:14:32.995
To transition that code, I
needed to know not only the

00:14:32.995 --> 00:14:35.815
Glmnet existed and was able
to perform regularization,

00:14:37.075 --> 00:14:40.515
but I needed to look at
the documentation to see how that

00:14:40.515 --> 00:14:41.575
function wanted

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:43.820
predictors to be formatted,

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:46.380
how it wanted the
outcome to be formatted,

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:50.065
whether it needed any
additional arguments which of

00:14:50.065 --> 00:14:53.985
the functions in the package
I needed to use to do so.

00:14:53.985 --> 00:14:58.000
And again, in tidymodels, all
I needed to do was change the

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:00.260
value of that engine argument.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:06.655
And the story goes the same way
with any other engine you can imagine.

00:15:07.435 --> 00:15:11.755
Let's say instead of Glmnet, I
wanted to use the H2O package,

00:15:11.755 --> 00:15:14.690
which gives me access
to all sorts of,

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:15.910
machine learning techniques

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:19.190
implemented in Java,
so it's super fast.

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:21.090
With tidymodels,

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:24.125
all I need to do is change
the value of engine to

00:15:24.125 --> 00:15:25.185
h20.

00:15:26.045 --> 00:15:30.205
But with h2o, I need
to initialize an h2o

00:15:30.205 --> 00:15:34.910
server I need to convert
that mtcars data set to a

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:38.110
data set that's
compatible with h2o using

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:39.675
as.h20

00:15:40.555 --> 00:15:44.655
and I assign a label to
that mtcars data set, the string

00:15:45.195 --> 00:15:49.460
mtcars, and then I pass that
string as the last argument to

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:51.960
h20s linear model function

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:54.200
h20.glm.

00:15:56.575 --> 00:16:00.495
Also, the h20.glm
function doesn't take the

00:16:00.495 --> 00:16:03.775
values of the predictors,
instead it takes the names.

00:16:03.775 --> 00:16:07.680
So I take the names
of the mtcars dataset,

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:10.640
the portion of it that
has the predictors in it,

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:12.720
and the same goes
for the outcome.

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:17.745
It takes the name of the outcome
in the mtcars dataset as a string.

00:16:19.485 --> 00:16:22.660
So all of this, change
in the code. Again,

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:24.920
I would need to spend time to,

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:27.080
read through the documentation,

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:31.645
see how that interface changes,
And only once I've done so,

00:16:31.645 --> 00:16:34.865
can I make use
of the functionality within H2O?

00:16:36.045 --> 00:16:37.260
And with tidymodels,

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:40.080
I just get that for
free by changing the engine argument.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:46.960
Another reason to
use tidymodels is safety.

00:16:48.145 --> 00:16:52.545
Tidymodels is designed to,
safeguard you from common

00:16:52.545 --> 00:16:53.525
modeling pitfalls.

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:57.880
One of the things that
tidymodels is designed to

00:16:57.880 --> 00:17:01.720
protect you from is data
leakage. A 2023 review found

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:04.825
data leakage to be a widespread
failure mode in machine

00:17:04.825 --> 00:17:06.045
learning based science.

00:17:06.825 --> 00:17:08.845
Data leakage is
when information

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:14.480
from the set you're going
to use or the portion of the

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:18.905
data that you're going to
use to evaluate your model somehow

00:17:18.905 --> 00:17:23.225
leaks into the portion of the data
that you're using to train your model.

00:17:23.225 --> 00:17:27.325
If a model is being evaluated
on data that it's seen already,

00:17:27.850 --> 00:17:33.130
then it will perform better than
on data that it hasn't seen before.

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:35.930
And so it will seem like your
model is performing really

00:17:35.930 --> 00:17:37.965
well, but it's actually not,

00:17:37.965 --> 00:17:41.325
and you'll only learn that
the model isn't performing well

00:17:41.325 --> 00:17:42.385
once it's deployed.

00:17:43.325 --> 00:17:44.465
So in our example,

00:17:45.300 --> 00:17:46.680
in the mutagen example,

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:51.000
if our model seems to
be able to classify

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:56.325
drugs as either mutagens or
non mutagens really, really well.

00:17:56.325 --> 00:18:00.005
If we go ahead and deploy that,
thinking that the model is

00:18:00.005 --> 00:18:02.585
performing perfectly, then,

00:18:03.765 --> 00:18:05.370
scientists will mistakenly

00:18:07.030 --> 00:18:10.150
interpret the results
from that model and,

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:11.530
take them at face value.

00:18:14.435 --> 00:18:16.855
Another common failure
mode is overfitting.

00:18:18.995 --> 00:18:21.635
Overfitting leads to analysts
believing models are more

00:18:21.635 --> 00:18:23.400
performant than
they actually are.

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:27.320
So this is really similar
to that first failure mode, where,

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:31.020
in the case of an overfit
model, in our mutagen example,

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:36.505
the model is going to perform
really, really well on,

00:18:37.705 --> 00:18:41.710
the portion of the data that
was used to train the model And

00:18:41.710 --> 00:18:45.710
if we don't have tools to
then evaluate the model on data that

00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:50.445
it hasn't seen before, then,
that model might just learn

00:18:50.445 --> 00:18:53.725
every little intricacy of
the training data and won't

00:18:53.725 --> 00:18:56.465
generalize well to data
that it hasn't seen before.

00:18:59.310 --> 00:19:02.270
A last component of safety that,

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:05.630
tidymodels makes sure to
protect its users from is

00:19:05.630 --> 00:19:06.130
irreproducibility

00:19:07.885 --> 00:19:08.385
implementations

00:19:09.085 --> 00:19:13.425
of the same machine learning
model give differing results in many,

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:17.350
popular implementations of
machine learning algorithms

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:21.190
resulting in irreproducibility
of modeling results.

00:19:22.425 --> 00:19:27.225
So each of the citations listed
in the bottom here in order are,

00:19:27.225 --> 00:19:30.665
reports on data leakage
and over fitting and

00:19:30.665 --> 00:19:31.165
irreproducibility.

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:35.790
And tidymodels is designed to
safeguard you from all of them.

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:40.570
We develop this software so
that it's really hard to do the

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:44.445
things that are wrong and
that an analyst would commonly want

00:19:44.445 --> 00:19:48.445
to do and makes the things
that are usually difficult,

00:19:48.445 --> 00:19:52.540
but pay off in the long it
makes those things really easy.

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:58.815
Another reason why you
might wanna use tidymodels,

00:19:58.815 --> 00:20:01.875
is that tidymodels is
designed to be communicable.

00:20:03.375 --> 00:20:04.850
All tidymodels objects,

00:20:04.850 --> 00:20:08.850
or at least many of
them have visualization functions called

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:11.430
autoplot that are
associated with them.

00:20:12.735 --> 00:20:14.435
So if I make a,

00:20:15.615 --> 00:20:18.335
receiver operating curve
with the tidy models,

00:20:18.335 --> 00:20:22.740
which is a tool used to,
evaluate models, I can pass

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:26.580
that object to autoplot and
see what that curve looks

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:28.280
like in 2D space.

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:32.985
I can also pass, for example,
the result of trying to tune a

00:20:32.985 --> 00:20:35.145
random forest algorithm. Again,

00:20:35.145 --> 00:20:37.945
I just passed that result
to the autoplot function,

00:20:37.945 --> 00:20:41.645
and we have built
in visualization procedures for that.

00:20:42.170 --> 00:20:43.070
Model object.

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:48.410
The same story goes
for iterative search results,

00:20:48.410 --> 00:20:50.190
things like bayesian
optimization,

00:20:50.975 --> 00:20:52.575
simulated annealing.

00:20:52.575 --> 00:20:56.335
These are advanced machine
learning techniques that are

00:20:56.335 --> 00:20:59.830
built into tidymodels When
I use tidymodels to generate

00:20:59.830 --> 00:21:04.010
these results, I can understand
them by quickly plotting them.

00:21:05.995 --> 00:21:09.055
Another example to use
tidymodels is completeness.

00:21:09.755 --> 00:21:13.760
There's built in support
for ninety nine machine models,

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:16.800
once you load the
tidymodels packages,

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:21.995
and there are even more
packages if you load extensions

00:21:21.995 --> 00:21:24.255
that are contributed
by the community.

00:21:26.635 --> 00:21:30.235
Tidymodels is also complete
with reference to data pre

00:21:30.235 --> 00:21:31.820
processing techniques.

00:21:31.820 --> 00:21:34.940
With the three main packages
for pre processing that are

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:36.880
loaded when you
load tidymodels,

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:41.855
we load in a hundred and two
different ways to process

00:21:41.855 --> 00:21:45.375
data that comes in raw and
turn it into something that a model

00:21:45.375 --> 00:21:46.595
knows what to do with.

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:52.220
And I've hinted at this
already, but again,

00:21:52.220 --> 00:21:53.360
tidymodels is extensible.

00:21:53.980 --> 00:21:57.125
If you can't find the
machine learning technique you need,

00:21:57.125 --> 00:22:00.405
which is either the machine
learning model or the pre

00:22:00.405 --> 00:22:04.140
processing technique, you
can add it yourself. We have

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:08.700
APIs that are designed to
make it really easy to contribute

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:11.900
your own extensions that
are designed with your problem

00:22:11.900 --> 00:22:13.440
context in mind.

00:22:16.045 --> 00:22:18.545
The last reason I'll list
here is deployability,

00:22:19.085 --> 00:22:22.750
and this will bring us into
that final step three that will

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:24.530
drop into the live demo for.

00:22:25.310 --> 00:22:27.950
Tidymodels is tightly
integrated with vetiver,

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:31.232
which is a free and
open source package for model deployment.

00:22:31.232 --> 00:22:36.375
And vetiver is configured to
quickly and easily and securely,

00:22:37.555 --> 00:22:39.690
ship a model to Posit Connect,

00:22:39.690 --> 00:22:43.150
which is part of the suite
of products in Posit Team.

